In dermoscopy melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with variegated colors—brown, black, blue-gray and red—featuring an atypical pigment network, irregular blotches, streaks, regression areas and peripheral dots.
On dermoscopy, melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with an irregular, spiculated pigment network, multiple colors (light to dark brown, black, blue-gray, white), atypical dots and globules.
Dermoscopic melanoma often appears as an asymmetrical lesion with irregular, poorly defined borders, heterogeneous pigmentation—black, brown, streaks or regression structures.
Dermoscopy of melanoma often shows an asymmetric, irregular pigment network with multiple colors—black, brown, blue-gray, white, and red—irregular dots or globules, with variable size and pattern.
In dermoscopic images, melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with irregular, scalloped or notched borders, heterogeneous pigmentation including black, brown, dots, and regression structures.
On dermoscopy melanoma often appears asymmetric with irregular, poorly demarcated borders, an atypical pigment network, uneven blotches, streaks and dots, and multiple colors.
In dermoscopy, melanoma often appears as an asymmetrical, irregularly pigmented lesion with uneven borders, multiple colors (black, brown, blue-gray, red), atypical network disruption.
In dermoscopy, melanoma appears as an irregular pigmented lesion with asymmetrical shape, uneven dark brown to black pigmentation, atypical network, irregular blotches, streaks.
In dermoscopy, melanoma often appears asymmetric with irregular, scalloped borders, heterogeneous colors (brown, black, blue-gray, red, white), atypical pigment network.
In dermoscopic images, melanoma appears as an irregular, multicolored lesion with asymmetrical borders, atypical pigment network, blue-white veil.
